Real-page JavaScript bundles, for `make bench-js` (host, tests/unit/js_bench.c),
`make bench-js-os` (on the machine, tests/unit/js_bench_guest.c) and
`make test-js-syntax` (tests/unit/js_syntax_test.c).

WHY THEY ARE COMMITTED RATHER THAN FETCHED
Same reason as tests/fixtures/cssperf: a compile-rate number is only comparable
against itself. A bundle fetched at run time changes under the measurement --
the site ships a new build, the CDN serves a different chunk -- and then a
"regression" is just a different program. These are the exact bytes the
before/after numbers in the commit messages were measured on. They also take
the network out of the timing, which is the point: fetching a bundle is the
HTTP layer's cost, compiling it is the engine's.

PROVENANCE (captured 2026-08-08, unmodified, exactly as served)

  kimi-index.mjs          1,623,655 B  https://statics.moonshot.cn/kimi-web-seo/assets/index-h6DE6Ow7.js
  kimi-polyfills.mjs         96,106 B  .../assets/polyfills-BmY-sjxE.js
      both are <script type="module"> on https://www.kimi.com/ -- ES modules,
      not classic scripts, and the big one is the largest single file here.
  baidu-async-search.js     805,094 B  https://pss.bdstatic.com/r/www/cache/static/protocol/https/global/js/all_async_search_f9d4473.js
  baidu-polyfill.js          41,984 B  https://pss.bdstatic.com/r/www/cache/static/protocol/https/bundles/polyfill_9354efa.js
  baidu-jquery-1.10.2.js    143,929 B  https://pss.bdstatic.com/static/superman/js/lib/jquery-1-edb203c114.10.2.js
      all three are classic <script src> on https://www.baidu.com/
  deepseek-6559.js          191,620 B  https://www.deepseek.com/_next/static/chunks/6559-c46c52cda76e1166.js
  deepseek-86b88eeb.js      171,906 B  .../chunks/86b88eeb-ec19e45782d2c43b.js
  deepseek-polyfills.js      91,460 B  .../chunks/polyfills-c67a75d1b6f99dc8.js
  bing-uCMySA2.js            34,498 B  https://www.bing.com/rp/uCMySA2WQ3cNt4c2N2n7j7QAXrE.js
      the largest of the fifteen scripts bing.com loads; the other fourteen
      total under 12 KB, which is itself the finding about that page.

WHY THIS SET, THEY ARE DIFFERENT SHAPES
  kimi-index      one 1.55 MB ES module: `import`/`export`, top-level `const`,
                  thousands of small arrow functions. The worst case for
                  compile latency on this machine, and the only fixture that
                  exercises the module path end to end.
  baidu-async     one 805 KB classic script, a single enormous IIFE. No module
                  syntax at all -- the shape a 2015-era build system emits.
  baidu-polyfill  42 KB of core-js-style feature detection. NEGATIVE CONTROL:
                  before the hex-literal lexer fix it did not compile AT ALL
                  (`SyntaxError: invalid number literal` on
                  `0xde0b6b3a7640080.toFixed(0)` at line 31), so every polyfill
                  in it was silently missing from the page.
  baidu-jquery    144 KB of hand-written, non-minified library code: long
                  identifiers, comments, real whitespace. The opposite input to
                  a minified chunk, and it separates lexer cost from parse cost.
  deepseek-*      webpack chunks off a Next.js app: dense minified React.
  bing-*          the small-script page. Included because a benchmark made only
                  of megabyte bundles measures the wrong end of the range.

RE-FETCHING (if they ever need refreshing -- the numbers must be re-measured)
  curl -A 'Mozilla/5.0' --compressed <url> -o <file>
  Send --compressed: at least one baidu host serves gzip whether or not you
  asked, and a gzip body compiles to "SyntaxError: unexpected token" rather
  than to anything informative.

These are third-party programs kept verbatim as test input only, and are not
part of LogitOS. jquery 1.10.2 is MIT (jQuery Foundation). The remaining files
are (c) their respective sites (Moonshot AI, Baidu, DeepSeek, Microsoft) and
are used here solely as fixed compiler input.
